    {
      "id": "emergent_compute_pact",
      "description": "A coalition of mid-sized EU member states, frustrated by the paralysis in Brussels and the repeated denial of sovereign access, begins quiet coordination with Japan, South Korea, and Canada to establish a mutual fallback network for compute access and model evaluation. The initiative is framed as 'resilience cooperation' but includes binding commitments to share capacity if any member is cut off by a great power. It gains momentum due to the ongoing suspension of the Emergency Sovereign Compute Directive and Poland’s earlier defection.",
      "first_turn": 5,
      "last_turn": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "emergent_safety_consortium",
      "description": "Following the US-China agreement and the EU's visible struggle to operationalize sovereignty, a group of European AI safety startups and university labs announce a joint open initiative—'EuroShield Next'—to develop automated monitoring tools for model misuse, funded by a mix of national grants and philanthropy. The project explicitly distances itself from government control but offers to feed findings into the stalled Sovereign Evaluation and Control Framework, creating a de facto parallel assurance system.",
      "first_turn": 5,
